## 1. Project Overview

CloudForge is a computer networking project that implements and evaluates a software-based HTTP load balancer in a controlled Linux networking environment.

The system distributes incoming HTTP requests across two application servers using different load-balancing strategies. It monitors backend health, excludes unavailable servers, reinstates recovered servers, and handles eligible request failures through bounded retry.

The project uses Linux network namespaces, virtual Ethernet interfaces, Linux bridges, static routing, two software routers, FastAPI application servers, and a shared PostgreSQL database.

The primary objective is to study how different load-balancing strategies behave under controlled network conditions, including delay, packet loss, bandwidth limitations, and backend failures.

The project also examines how network-level conditions influence application-level performance through controlled experiments, workload generation, statistical analysis, and packet-level inspection.

## 2. Objectives

The main objectives are to:

- Implement HTTP request forwarding through a central gateway.
- Implement Round Robin (RR) load balancing.
- Implement Least Outstanding Requests (LOR) load balancing.
- Monitor backend availability through periodic readiness checks.
- Exclude unhealthy backends and reinstate recovered servers.
- Implement bounded retry for eligible read requests.
- Prevent unsafe automatic retries of write operations.
- Construct a multi-subnet routed Linux networking environment.
- Apply controlled network delay, packet loss, and bandwidth limitations.
- Measure performance under different network conditions.
- Analyze packet-level communication using Wireshark.
- Compare load-balancing strategies using repeatable experimental results.

