## 6. Experimental Methodology

The project compares Round Robin and Least Outstanding Requests under controlled network conditions.

Every formal measured run uses:

- 10 seconds of warm-up.
- 60 seconds of measurement.
- Five concurrent worker threads.
- One persistent HTTP client connection per worker.
- GET requests to the gateway's `/users/` endpoint.
- A 5-second request timeout.

### Workload Generator Configuration

The workload is generated by `scripts/workload.py` using a fixed-concurrency, closed-loop request model.

The five-worker configuration was selected from preliminary RR and LOR calibration runs and held constant across comparable formal experiments.

Calibration runs are separate from the formal evaluation.

### 6.1 Completed Formal Experiments

All 63 planned formal runs have been completed. Each condition has three repetitions.

| Experiment | Conditions | Policies | Repetitions | Runs |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|
| Added round-trip delay | 20, 100, 200 ms | RR and LOR | 3 | 18 |
| Packet loss | 0%, 2%, 5% | RR and LOR | 3 | 18 |
| Bandwidth | 100, 20, 5 Mbit/s | RR and LOR | 3 | 18 |
| Backend failure and recovery | 20, 100, 200 ms delay | LOR | 3 | 9 |
| **Total** | | | | **63** |

**Formal evaluation: 63 measured runs across 21 experimental conditions.**

Each measured run uses a 10-second warm-up followed by a 60-second measurement period.

Calibration observations and preliminary workload-validation runs are not counted as formal measured runs.

#### Network Impairment Configuration

- **Delay:** Applied on the R1-to-R2 transit egress interface. The configured one-way delay produces approximately the corresponding added RTT for a round-trip ping.
- **Packet loss:** Applied on the R1-to-R2 egress direction.
- **Bandwidth:** Shaping applied to both R1-to-R2 and R2-to-R1 transit interfaces.

Delay and packet-loss conditions were calibrated using ICMP measurements.

Bandwidth conditions were calibrated using iperf3.

The workload consists of small HTTP GET requests. Consequently, these experiments measure application-level request performance under configured network conditions; they should not be interpreted as bulk-link saturation measurements.

### 6.2 Evaluation Metrics

The formal performance analysis reports:

- Successful requests per second.
- Client-observed mean latency for successful HTTP 2xx requests.
- Client-observed p50 latency for successful HTTP 2xx requests.
- Client-observed p95 latency for successful HTTP 2xx requests.
- HTTP error rate and failed-request counts.
- Supplementary all-attempt latency statistics, including failed requests.
- Backend request distribution.
- Mean and sample standard deviation across the three repetitions.

The failure/recovery experiments additionally record successful throughput, latency, errors, HTTP 502 responses, backend distribution, and observed health-state transitions.

Actual RTT was measured during impairment calibration.

Exact backend failure-detection and recovery durations were not instrumented as precise elapsed-time metrics. TCP retransmissions have not yet been quantified as a formal evaluation metric.

