#### Performance Observations

- Under 20 ms and 100 ms added delay, LOR achieved higher average throughput and lower average latency than RR in these experiments.
- Under 200 ms delay, both policies produced very similar results, with RR marginally ahead in throughput.
- Under the 0% packet-loss baseline, both policies performed similarly.
- At 2% packet loss, RR achieved higher average throughput, while LOR showed greater run-to-run variability.
- At 5% packet loss, LOR achieved higher average throughput and lower mean and p95 latency.
- At 20 Mbit/s bandwidth, both policies produced nearly identical results.
- At 100 Mbit/s, RR had higher average throughput but greater variability.
- At 5 Mbit/s, LOR produced higher average throughput and more consistent results.

These observations describe the measured workload and test environment. They do not establish that one policy is universally superior.

Some conditions exhibited considerable run-to-run variation. All valid repetitions have been retained rather than selectively excluding slower runs.

### 6.4 Backend Failure and Recovery Results

Nine failure/recovery experiments were conducted using the LOR policy, with three repetitions at each configured delay.

| Delay | Successful RPS (mean ± SD) | Mean Latency (ms, mean ± SD) | Average p95 (ms) | Error Rate (mean %) | Total HTTP 502 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 20 ms | 110.239 ± 2.673 | 45.336 ± 1.093 | 63.459 | 0.020 | 4 |
| 100 ms | 38.278 ± 0.308 | 130.545 ± 1.000 | 146.529 | 0.116 | 8 |
| 200 ms | 21.567 ± 0.060 | 231.638 ± 0.891 | 244.146 | 0.154 | 6 |

During these experiments:

1. Both backends initially operated normally.
2. Backend A was stopped during the measurement period.
3. The gateway detected its unavailability and marked it unhealthy.
4. Requests continued through Backend B.
5. Backend A was restarted.
6. The gateway observed consecutive successful readiness probes and marked it healthy again.

A small number of HTTP 502 responses occurred during failure transitions. These are included in the measured error rates and have not been excluded.

All nine runs recorded both unhealthy and recovery transitions.

The exact elapsed failure-detection and recovery durations were not measured, so no precise timing claim is made.

