## 14. Limitations and Scope

CloudForge is a controlled academic networking laboratory, not a production-grade cloud load-balancing platform.

The project does not aim to implement:

- High availability for the gateway itself.
- High availability for PostgreSQL.
- TLS termination.
- Automatic cloud autoscaling.
- Kubernetes deployment.
- Production-grade distributed monitoring.
- Automatic database transaction recovery.

The gateway and shared PostgreSQL database remain potential single points of failure.

The performance evaluation uses a fixed-concurrency, closed-loop workload of small HTTP GET requests. Results therefore characterize the tested application and network configuration rather than all possible workloads.

Only three repetitions were performed per formal condition. Run-to-run variability is reported using sample standard deviation, and measured outliers have been retained.

Failure/recovery tests include a small number of transition-related HTTP 502 responses. Exact failure-detection and recovery durations were not instrumented.

## 15. Conclusion

CloudForge implements and evaluates software-based HTTP load balancing using real Linux networking components.

The system combines two application backends, a central gateway, health monitoring, dynamic request distribution, bounded retry, a shared PostgreSQL database, and a routed multi-subnet Linux environment.

Both Round Robin and Least Outstanding Requests were implemented and verified. The gateway was tested under backend failure and recovery, and its bounded retry behavior was verified using isolated and live routed tests.

The formal evaluation comprises 63 measured runs across delay, packet-loss, bandwidth, and backend failure/recovery conditions. The results have been validated and summarized using means and sample standard deviations. Four PCAPNG captures and functional verification logs provide additional packet-level and implementation evidence.

The remaining work focuses on annotated Wireshark screenshots, reproducible topology automation, the technical report, demonstration recording, and final submission review.

